    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Prove that the intervals joining the midpoints of the sides of a triangle dissect the triangle into four congruent triangles, each similar to the original triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The four smaller triangles are congruent by SSS, and each is similar to the original triangle with scale factor one half.",
      "worked": "Let M, N and P be the midpoints of AB, BC and CA. By question1, $MN=\\frac{AC}{2}$, $MP=\\frac{BC}{2}$ and $NP=\\frac{AB}{2}$. Each of the four triangles AMP, MBN, PNC and NPM therefore has the three side lengths $\\frac{AB}{2}$, $\\frac{BC}{2}$ and $\\frac{AC}{2}$. Hence all four are congruent by SSS. In each, the corresponding sides are in the ratio1:2 to the sides of ABC, so each is similar to ABC by SSS.",
